<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Lesson Overview</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01460</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Lesson Overview</md:title>
    <md:uuid>a763e9f7-7933-41e9-9938-eb347e69487b</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><para id="para-00001">In this lesson, you will analyze the graph of an exponential function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> given by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>. In particular, you study the effect of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> on the shape of the graph. To observe how <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> impacts the shape of the graph when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> and when <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, you simultaneously examine several functions of this form, all of which have the same value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
<para id="para-00002">These lessons encourage greater attention to the growth factors of the different functions, rather than on interpreting the quantities in context. Since you are focusing mainly on understanding how varying <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> impacts the graph, you are looking at the structure of an exponential function and its graph.</para>
<para id="para-00003">When you finish this lesson, you will be able to:</para>
<list id="list-00001">
  <item>Describe the effect of changing <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> on a graph that represents <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>.</item>
  <item>Use equations and graphs to compare exponential functions.</item>
</list>
<para id="para-00004">Here are the <emphasis>activities</emphasis> that will help you reach those goals:</para>
<list id="list-00002">
  <item>5.12.1: Modeling Exponential Decay</item>
  <item>5.12.2: Equations and Their Graphs <list id="list-00003">
      <item>5.12.2: Self Check</item>

      <item>5.12.2: Additional Resources</item>
    </list>
 </item>
  <item>5.12.3: Graphs Representing Exponential Decay <list id="list-00005">
      <item>5.12.3: Self Check</item>
      <item>5.12.2: Additional Resources</item>
    </list>
 </item>
  <item>5.12.4: Possible Equation for a Function on a Graph</item>
</list>
      <para id="para-00065">After these activities, you completed the following <emphasis>practice</emphasis> and <emphasis>review</emphasis>:</para>
      <list id="list-00007">
        <item>5.12.5: Practice</item>
        <item>5.12.6: Lesson Summary</item>
      </list>
</section>
</content></document>